Chapter 2

Literature Review

2.1 The Battery Fallacy and Ragone Plots
The empirical trade‑off between specific energy and specific power has been codified since David Ragone introduced his eponymous plot (Ragone, “Thermodynamic considerations in the design of energy conversion devices”, 1968). Christen and Carlen (“Theory of Ragone plots”, J. Power Sources, 2000) later provided a differential‑equation framework. Despite these insights, the fallacy that a single superior chemistry will eventually emerge persists. This thesis elevates the Ragone observation to a theorem, generalising it to any dissipative storage system.

2.2 Screening Curves in Power Systems
Capacity expansion planners have used screening curves—plots of annualised cost against capacity factor—to determine the optimal mix of base‑load, intermediate, and peaking generators (Turvey & Anderson, Electricity Economics, 1977; Stoughton, Power System Planning, 1980). We adapt this logic to the duration domain, treating storage duration τ as the analog of capacity factor.

2.3 Long‑Duration Energy Storage Economics
Sepulveda et al. (“The design space for long‑duration energy storage in decarbonized power systems”, Nature Energy, 2021) mapped the LDES design space, showing that capital costs per kWh must fall below ~20 $ / kWh to significantly reduce electricity costs, and below ~1 $ / kWh to fully displace firm generation. The U.S. Department of Energy’s Long Duration Storage Shot (DOE, Storage Innovations 2030, 2022) quantified cost targets and learning rates for ten LDES technologies, with implementation durations of 5.5–11 years. These trajectories inform our dynamic envelope.

2.4 Multi‑Timescale Storage Control
Hierarchical model predictive control (MPC) for hybrid storage has been demonstrated by Zhang et al. (2022, Applied Energy), Li et al. (2023, IEEE Trans. Smart Grid), and Park et al. (2023, Energy), achieving operational cost reductions of 7–61% relative to rule‑based or single‑layer MPC. These results motivate our definition of coordination efficiency κ.

2.5 Reliability Theory with Correlated Failures
Classical reliability block diagrams assume independence, but common‑cause failures are well documented. IEC 61508 (2010) and the N‑1‑1 criterion (NERC, Reliability Standards, 2022) address correlated outages. Copula‑based multivariate failure models (Nelsen, An Introduction to Copulas, 2006) offer a modern framework; we incorporate them with dynamic extensions.

2.6 Experience Curves and Technological Learning
Empirical learning curves show substantial heterogeneity. PV modules and Li‑ion batteries have seen >90% cost reduction since 1990 with learning rates of 20–25%, while pumped hydro exhibits near‑zero learning (McNerney et al., Energy Policy, 2021). Wind energy showed epochs of accelerated learning (40–45%) interspersed with stagnation. Our SDEs capture technology‑specific, epoch‑dependent rates.

2.7 Exergy Analysis and Conversion Losses
Multi‑stage conversion chains suffer severe cumulative losses. Electricity–hydrogen–electricity round‑trip efficiency is ~36% (Pivovar et al., Int. J. Hydrogen Energy, 2023). In a multi‑technology green hydrogen system, electrolysis alone dissipates 39% of total input exergy (Gerloff, Energy, 2023). The coordination layer must explicitly model exergy destruction.

2.8 The Self‑Cannibalism Effect
In markets with high storage penetration, each new unit reduces the full‑load hours (FLH) of existing units. An Austrian case study (Haas et al., Applied Energy, 2022) found that FLH below 1500 h/yr render most storage technologies unprofitable. This effect necessitates a joint deployment constraint in portfolio optimisation.

