Chapter 3

Theorem 1: The Generalised Ragone Limit and the Impossibility of a Universal Storage Device

3.1 Introduction
We prove that any storage device with nonzero internal dissipation cannot simultaneously deliver its full energy capacity and its maximum power. The proof is given first for the linear Thévenin circuit, then extended to any dissipative system using passivity‑based arguments.

3.2 Thévenin Model Derivation
Model a generic storage device as an ideal electromotive force ε in series with an internal resistance R. Discharging at constant power P into a load, Kirchhoff’s voltage law yields

P = ε I – R I²  ⇒  R I² – ε I + P = 0.   (3.1)

For real solutions, ε² – 4RP ≥ 0 ⇒ P ≤ P_max = ε²/(4R). The lower‑current root (stable operation) is

I₋(P) = (ε – √(ε² – 4RP)) / (2R).   (3.2)

If the device stores total charge Q, the discharge duration at constant current I is Q/I, and the energy delivered at constant power P is

E(P) = P·Q / I₋(P) = Q f(P),   f(P) = P / I₋(P).

Theorem 1 (Ragone Limit, Linear Case). E(P) is strictly decreasing on (0, P_max], with E(P_max) = ½ E(0⁺).

Proof. Differentiate (3.1) implicitly: (2RI – ε) dI/dP + 1 = 0 ⇒ dI/dP = 1/(ε – 2RI). On the lower branch, I < ε/(2R) so dI/dP > 0. Now f′(P) = (I – P I′)/I². Multiply numerator by (ε – 2RI) and substitute P = εI – RI²:

I(ε – 2RI) – P = Iε – 2RI² – (εI – RI²) = –RI² < 0.

Since (ε – 2RI) > 0, f′(P) < 0. Hence E(P) = Q f(P) strictly decreases. At P→0⁺, expansion gives I₋ ≈ P/ε, so E(0⁺) = Qε. At P = P_max, I = ε/(2R) and E(P_max) = Qε/2. ∎

Even under idealised linear losses, a storage device delivers at most half its nominal energy when run at maximum power. Practical devices degrade faster.

3.3 Generalisation via Dissipativity
Let the device have stored exergy S(t) ≥ 0, power output P_out(t) ≥ 0, and dissipation rate Φ(t) ≥ 0. Exergy balance during discharge:

Ṡ(t) = –P_out(t) – Φ(t).   (3.3)

The maximum stored exergy is S_max (fully charged); S = 0 when empty. The device can sustain output only up to some P_max.

Generalised Theorem 1. If Φ(t) is strictly positive for all P_out > 0 and is an increasing function of the current power level (so that the average dissipation \bar{Φ}(P) increases with P), then the total extractable energy E(P) at constant power P is strictly decreasing, and E(P) → 0 as P → P_max unless Φ can be made arbitrarily small at high power—which is inconsistent with known physical kinetics.

Proof. Discharge at constant P for time t_f until S(t_f)=0. Integrate (3.3):

S_max = P t_f + ∫₀^{t_f} Φ(t) dt.

Define the time‑average dissipation \bar{Φ}(P) = (1/t_f)∫₀^{t_f} Φ(t) dt. Then S_max = (P + \bar{Φ}(P)) t_f, giving

t_f = S_max / (P + \bar{Φ}(P)),   E(P) = P t_f = [P/(P + \bar{Φ}(P))] S_max.   (3.4)

Since \bar{Φ}(P) is strictly positive and increases with P (Ohmic losses scale as P², overpotentials increase nonlinearly, etc.), the fraction P/(P+\bar{Φ}) strictly decreases. If \bar{Φ} → ∞ as P → P_max, E(P) → 0. ∎

Thus the Ragone trade‑off is a fundamental consequence of irreversibility. This result does not depend on gravitational or thermal environment; it holds equally on the lunar surface.

3.4 Connection to Cost Structure
We will later argue that devices with high power density p_i (low F_i ∝ 1/p_i) must have low deliverable energy at that power, and physical design trade‑offs correlate this with low volumetric energy density e_i. Consequently, a low‑F device tends to have high V_i, and a low‑V device tends to have high F_i. This populates a Pareto frontier in (F,V) space, ensuring that the cost lines cross and the fractal partition is nontrivial. Theorem 1 provides a physically motivated rationale, not a formal deduction.

