Chapter 5

Theorem 3: Diversity, Redundancy, and Dynamic Reliability

5.1 Independent Layers and Exponential Reliability Gain
Consider a single duration band served by m independent storage layers, each capable of meeting the full demand of that band. Let A_j be the availability of layer j.

Theorem 3 (Independent Redundancy). The probability that all m layers fail simultaneously is P_fail = ∏{j=1}^m (1 – A_j). This product strictly decreases in m for each additional layer with A{m+1} > 0. If A_j = A for all j, P_fail = (1–A)^m, decaying exponentially in m.

Proof. By independence, the joint failure probability factorises; each factor is in (0,1), so the product strictly decreases. ∎

5.2 Correlated Failures and Common‑Cause Shocks
Introduce a common‑cause shock with probability γ that fails all layers simultaneously; with probability 1–γ, failures are independent with probabilities λ_j. The system failure probability becomes

P_fail = γ + (1–γ) ∏_{j=1}^m (1 – λ_j).   (5.1)

For γ > 0, P_fail is bounded below by γ, so diversity benefit saturates.

5.3 Dynamic Copula‑Based Correlation
Let the failure indicator for layer j be X_j(t) ∈ {0,1}, with marginal failure probabilities F_j(t). The joint probability of all layers failing at time t is given by a copula C:

P(X_1=0,…,X_m=0; t) = C_{Σ(t)}(F_1(t),…,F_m(t)),

where Σ(t) is a time‑varying correlation matrix. For a Gaussian copula with uniform correlation ρ, the diversification benefit diminishes rapidly as ρ increases. At ρ = 1, adding layers yields no improvement.

Proposition 5.1 (Diminishing Returns Under Correlation). For a Gaussian copula with uniform correlation ρ and identical marginal availabilities A, the marginal reliability gain from adding the (m+1)-th layer is approximately

ΔR_{m+1} ≈ (1 – ρ) R_m A,

where R_m = 1 – P_fail^{(m)} is the system reliability with m layers. The benefit vanishes as ρ→1. Beyond a critical correlation ρ_crit = 1 – (C_{m+1}/C_m) / (R_m A), the cost of an additional layer exceeds the expected outage‑cost saving, and further layers are uneconomic.

Heuristic derivation. For a Gaussian copula with exchangeable correlation ρ, the joint survival function can be written using the representation

C̅(u_1,…,u_m) = Φ_m(Φ⁻¹(1-u_1),…,Φ⁻¹(1-u_m); Σ),   Σ = (1-ρ)I + ρ 1 1^T.

Using the conditional normal distribution, the probability that all m+1 layers survive given that the first m survive is approximately (1–ρ) times the marginal survival probability of the new layer, plus a term of order ρ. Therefore the incremental survival gain scales as (1–ρ). Equating the annualised cost of the new layer with the product of the avoided outage probability and the value of lost load yields the critical correlation threshold. A rigorous proof requires numerical evaluation of the multivariate normal orthant probability. ∎

5.4 Partial Substitutability and k‑out‑of‑m Reliability
If layer j can only supply a fraction α_j of the band’s demand, the system survives if at least k out of m layers are operational. The failure probability is then:

P_fail = 1 – ∑{l=k}^m ∑{\text{subsets of size } l} P(\text{exactly those } l \text{ layers up}).   (5.2)

Under correlated failures, the copula approach generalises to evaluate the survival function.

5.5 Decoupling Score
We propose a decoupling score δ_{ij} ∈ [0,1] for each technology pair, quantifying independence in failure mechanisms (different supply chains, control software, physical failure modes, geographical dispersion). The correlation parameter can be mapped as ρ_{ij} = 1 – δ_{ij}. High δ indicates effective diversity; low δ warns of limited reliability gain.

