Chapter 6

The Coordination Layer: Exergy‑Aware Nested Stochastic Control

6.1 The Challenge of Multi‑Layer Dispatch
Given n storage layers partitioned by duration, the operational task is a nested stochastic optimal control problem. Moving energy across duration bands incurs cascading thermodynamic penalties, captured by a conversion efficiency matrix.

6.2 Conversion Efficiency Matrix
Let η_{ij} ∈ (0,1] be the round‑trip efficiency of converting energy from the form native to layer i to the form of layer j. For electricity‑to‑electricity within the same type, η_{ii}=1 (device‑own round‑trip is separate). For electricity‑to‑hydrogen‑to‑electricity, η_{ij} ≈ 0.36. Component efficiencies (AC/DC 0.98, electrolysis 0.60, fuel cell 0.60, compression 0.90) construct the full matrix η.

6.3 State Equations and Power Balance
For layer i, let p_i⁺(t) be charging from grid, p_i⁻(t) discharging to grid, and p_{i→j}(t) power sent for conversion. The state of charge evolves as:

SoC_i(t+1) = SoC_i(t) + η_i⁺ p_i⁺(t) – p_i⁻(t)/η_i⁻ + ∑{j≠i} (η{ji} p_{j→i}(t) – p_{i→j}(t)/η_{ij}),   (6.1)

with 0 ≤ SoC_i(t) ≤ Cap_i. The global power balance, expressed at the electricity interface, is most transparently written with explicit conversion losses:

∑i p_i⁻(t) + L(t) = D(t) – G(t) + ∑i p_i⁺(t) + ∑{i,j} (1/η{ij} – 1) p_{i→j}(t).   (6.2)

Here the last term represents the additional grid power required to compensate for conversion losses when transferring energy from layer i to layer j. This formulation cleanly separates useful discharge from the parasitic demand of cross‑band transfers.

6.4 Exergy‑Based Objective and Decomposition of Coordination Efficiency
We define three dispatch benchmarks, all respecting device and conversion efficiencies:

· Structurally ideal dispatch (C_struct): energy is served entirely within its native duration band; no inter‑band transfers are permitted. The load‑duration curve is partitioned so that each band’s demand is met only by its designated technology.
· Perfect‑forecast dispatch (C_forecast): perfect foresight of all exogenous variables (demand, renewable generation) is available; inter‑band transfers are allowed when beneficial.
· Real dispatch (C_real): the outcome of the hierarchical MPC with realistic forecasts.

Coordination efficiency is decomposed as

κ_exergy = κ_forecast × κ_struct,

where κ_forecast = C_forecast / C_real (information component) and κ_struct = C_struct / C_forecast (thermodynamic component). A high κ_struct implies minimal exergy destruction from cross‑band routing; a high κ_forecast implies effective use of forecasts.

6.5 Nested Hierarchical Model Predictive Control
We adopt a three‑timescale MPC consistent with Zhang et al. (2022) and Li et al. (2023):

· Day‑ahead: commits strategic reserves, hydrogen schedules, long‑duration setpoints using coarse forecasts.
· Intraday: updates battery and flow battery setpoints every 15–60 min, respecting day‑ahead commitments.
· Real‑time: supercapacitors and flywheels absorb sub‑second to minute imbalances using local droop control or short‑horizon MPC.

At each layer, optimisation uses a shrinking or receding horizon with chance constraints. The day‑ahead problem anticipates the expected cost of intraday and real‑time recourse, forming a consistent stochastic program.

