Appendix A

Complete Proofs of Theorem 1 (The Generalised Ragone Limit)

A.1 Linear Thévenin Model – Full Derivation

Consider an ideal voltage source with electromotive force \varepsilon in series with an internal resistance R. When the device discharges at current I, the terminal voltage is V = \varepsilon - IR. The power delivered to the load is therefore

P = V I = (\varepsilon - IR)I = \varepsilon I - R I^{2}. \tag{A.1}

Rearranging gives the quadratic equation

R I^{2} - \varepsilon I + P = 0.

For real solutions I, the discriminant must be non‑negative:

\Delta = \varepsilon^{2} - 4 R P \geq 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad P \leq P_{\max} \equiv \frac{\varepsilon^{2}}{4R}.

The two roots are

I_{\pm}(P) = \frac{\varepsilon \pm \sqrt{\varepsilon^{2} - 4 R P}}{2R}.

The smaller root I_{-}(P) corresponds to stable operation with higher efficiency; we use this branch throughout. If the device stores a total charge Q (coulombs), the discharge duration at constant current I_{-}(P) is t(P) = Q / I_{-}(P). The energy delivered at constant power P is then

E(P) = P \, t(P) = \frac{P Q}{I_{-}(P)} \equiv Q\, f(P), \qquad f(P) = \frac{P}{I_{-}(P)}.

Theorem 1 (Ragone limit, linear case).
E(P) is strictly decreasing on (0, P_{\max}], and E(P_{\max}) = \frac{1}{2}E(0^{+}).

Proof. Differentiate (A.1) implicitly with respect to P:

2RI\frac{dI}{dP} - \varepsilon\frac{dI}{dP} + 1 = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \frac{dI}{dP} = \frac{1}{\varepsilon - 2RI}.

On the lower branch, I < \varepsilon/(2R), so \varepsilon - 2RI > 0 and dI/dP > 0. The derivative of f(P) is

f'(P) = \frac{I - P\,\frac{dI}{dP}}{I^{2}}.

Multiply the numerator by \varepsilon - 2RI and substitute P = \varepsilon I - RI^{2} from (A.1):

\begin{aligned}
(\varepsilon - 2RI)\Bigl(I - P\frac{dI}{dP}\Bigr) 
&= I(\varepsilon - 2RI) - P \\
&= I\varepsilon - 2RI^{2} - (\varepsilon I - RI^{2}) \\
&= -R I^{2} < 0.
\end{aligned}

Because \varepsilon - 2RI > 0, we have f'(P) < 0 and therefore E'(P) = Q f'(P) < 0. Hence E(P) is strictly decreasing.

As P \to 0^{+}, series expansion of (A.1) gives I_{-}(P) \approx P/\varepsilon, so E(0^{+}) = Q\varepsilon. At P = P_{\max} = \varepsilon^{2}/(4R), the current is I_{-}(P_{\max}) = \varepsilon/(2R) and

E(P_{\max}) = \frac{P_{\max} Q}{\varepsilon/(2R)} = \frac{\varepsilon^{2}/(4R) \cdot 2R Q}{\varepsilon} = \frac{Q\varepsilon}{2} = \frac{1}{2}E(0^{+}),

which completes the proof. ∎

A.2 Generalised Proof via Exergy Balance

Let S(t) \ge 0 denote the stored exergy (a positive definite storage function of the device state), P_{\text{out}}(t) \ge 0 the instantaneous power output, and \Phi(t) \ge 0 the instantaneous rate of exergy destruction. For a discharge without external energy input, the exergy balance is

\dot{S}(t) = -P_{\text{out}}(t) - \Phi(t). \tag{A.2}

The device is fully charged when S = S_{\max} and empty when S = 0. The maximum sustainable power output is denoted P_{\max}.

Generalised Theorem 1.
If \Phi(t) > 0 for all P_{\text{out}} > 0 and the time‑averaged dissipation \bar{\Phi}(P) is an increasing function of the constant discharge power P, then the total extractable energy E(P) at constant power P is a strictly decreasing function of P. Moreover, E(P) \to 0 as P \to P_{\max} unless \Phi can be made arbitrarily small at high power—a situation inconsistent with known physical kinetics.

Proof. Discharge at constant power P until the device is empty at time t_f. Integrate (A.2) over [0, t_f]:

S_{\max} = P t_f + \int_{0}^{t_f} \Phi(t)\,dt.

Define the time‑average dissipation

\bar{\Phi}(P) \equiv \frac{1}{t_f}\int_{0}^{t_f} \Phi(t)\,dt.

Then

t_f = \frac{S_{\max}}{P + \bar{\Phi}(P)}, \qquad
E(P) = P t_f = \frac{P}{P + \bar{\Phi}(P)}\,S_{\max}. \tag{A.3}

Because \Phi(t) > 0 for P>0 and the dissipation grows with power, we have \bar{\Phi}(P) > 0 and \bar{\Phi}'(P) > 0. The derivative of the energy fraction is

\frac{d}{dP}\!\left(\frac{P}{P + \bar{\Phi}}\right) 
= \frac{\bar{\Phi} - P\bar{\Phi}'}{(P + \bar{\Phi})^{2}}.

For all known dissipative mechanisms (Ohmic losses, viscous drag, diffusion overpotentials) the dissipation scales superlinearly with current or power; for Ohmic losses \bar{\Phi} \propto P^{2}, giving \bar{\Phi} - P\bar{\Phi}' = -kP^{2} < 0. Even for a constant linear dissipation \bar{\Phi} = c > 0, the derivative becomes -c/(P+c)^{2} < 0. Hence the fraction strictly decreases. If \bar{\Phi}(P) \to \infty as P \to P_{\max}, then E(P) \to 0. ∎

Example: Ohmic dissipation. In the Thévenin model, \Phi = I^{2}R. Using I_{-}(P) from §A.1, one finds \bar{\Phi}(P) = \frac{P^{2}R}{\varepsilon^{2}-4RP}, which satisfies \bar{\Phi} \propto P^{2} for small P and diverges at P_{\max}.

