Appendix B

Full Proof of Theorem 2 and Corollary 2.1 (Dynamic Optimal Duration Partition)

B.1 Static Partition – Proof of Theorem 2

Each storage technology i is characterised by an annualised cost function

C_{i}(\tau) = F_{i} + V_{i}\tau,

where F_{i} > 0 is the power‑capacity cost ($/kW‑yr) and V_{i} > 0 the energy‑capacity cost slope ($/kWh). All V_{i} are assumed distinct. The lower envelope is

\mathcal{E}(\tau) = \min_{i} C_{i}(\tau).

Theorem 2 (Static optimal duration partition).
\mathcal{E}(\tau) is continuous, piecewise‑linear, and concave. It partitions the duration axis [0,T] into at most n contiguous intervals, each served optimally by exactly one technology. Technologies are ordered from low F/high V at short durations to high F/low V at long durations.

Proof. Each C_{i} is affine, hence concave. The pointwise minimum of concave functions is concave. Two distinct lines C_{i} and C_{j} intersect at most once, at

\tau_{ij} = \frac{F_{j} - F_{i}}{V_{i} - V_{j}}.

The lower envelope selects the minimum value; therefore the technology that achieves the minimum changes only at these intersection points. With n technologies there are at most n-1 distinct breakpoints, yielding at most n intervals. Because all slopes V_{i} > 0, as \tau \to 0 the ranking is by the intercept F_{i} (smallest wins), and as \tau \to \infty the ranking is by the slope V_{i} (smallest wins). Hence the sequence of optimal technologies from short to long duration is from low F/high V to high F/low V. ∎

B.2 Microfoundation of the Pareto Frontier

The intercept F_{i} is inversely related to power density p_{i} (kW/kg or kW/m³): higher power density reduces the amount of material per kW and thus the capital cost. The slope V_{i} is inversely related to energy density e_{i}: higher energy density reduces the cost of storage media per kWh. Theorem 1 demonstrates that a device cannot simultaneously deliver its full energy at maximum power and possess high energy density, because design choices that lower internal resistance (thin electrodes, high porosity) reduce the volume fraction of active storage material. Consequently, a low‑F technology must have a high V, and a low‑V technology must have a high F. The set \{(F_{i}, V_{i})\} therefore lies on a Pareto frontier, guaranteeing that the cost lines cross and the duration partition is nontrivial.

B.3 Dynamic Envelope with Experience‑Curve Learning

Let Q_{i}(t) be the cumulative deployed capacity of technology i. The cost trajectories are modelled as stochastic processes:

\frac{dF_{i}}{F_{i}} = -\alpha_{i}^{F}\,\frac{dQ_{i}}{Q_{i}} + \sigma_{i}^{F}\,dW_{t}^{F},\qquad
\frac{dV_{i}}{V_{i}} = -\alpha_{i}^{V}\,\frac{dQ_{i}}{Q_{i}} + \sigma_{i}^{V}\,dW_{t}^{V},

where \alpha_{i}^{F}, \alpha_{i}^{V} \in [0,1] are learning elasticities and W_{t}^{F}, W_{t}^{V} are independent Wiener processes capturing R&D uncertainty, supply chain shocks, and policy changes. For a deterministic growth path Q_{i}(t) = Q_{i}(0)e^{g_{i}t}, the solution is

F_{i}(t) = F_{i}(0)\left(\frac{Q_{i}(t)}{Q_{i}(0)}\right)^{-\alpha_{i}^{F}}
\exp\!\Bigl(\sigma_{i}^{F}W_{t}^{F} - \tfrac{1}{2}(\sigma_{i}^{F})^{2}t\Bigr),

and analogously for V_{i}. The time‑dependent envelope \mathcal{E}(\tau,t) = \min_{i}[F_{i}(t) + V_{i}(t)\tau] then evolves, and the breakpoints \tau_{ij}(t) = [F_{j}(t) - F_{i}(t)]/[V_{i}(t) - V_{j}(t)] become random variables.

B.4 Corollary 2.1 – Constrained Partition with the Self‑Cannibalism Constraint

Empirical evidence indicates that storage assets become uneconomic when annual full‑load hours fall below \tau_{\min} \approx 1500\,\text{h}. We impose for every deployed technology

\mathrm{FLH}_{i}(S) \ge \tau_{\min}, \qquad
\mathrm{FLH}_{i}(S) \equiv \frac{1}{S_{i}}\int_{0}^{T} p_{i}^{-}(t)\,dt,

where S = (S_{1},\dots,S_{n}) is the vector of installed power capacities and p_{i}^{-}(t) is the dispatch of technology i. The cost‑minimisation becomes a bilevel problem because the dispatch depends on the capacities. We adopt a first‑order approximation that treats the representative band durations \tau_{i}^{*} as fixed by the unconstrained envelope, while the breakpoints can shift through capacity adjustments.

Corollary 2.1 (Constrained optimal duration partition).
Under the FLH constraint, the optimal portfolio still partitions the duration axis, but the breakpoints are compressed relative to the unconstrained case. The new breakpoints satisfy

\tau_{ij}^{\text{const}} = \tau_{ij}^{\text{unc}} - \delta_{ij}, \qquad
\delta_{ij} = \frac{\mu_{i} - \mu_{j}}{V_{j} - V_{i}},

where \mu_{k} \ge 0 is the Lagrange multiplier (shadow price) associated with the FLH constraint of technology k. Technologies that would otherwise dominate see their admissible duration band reduced.

Proof sketch. Formulate the Lagrangian of the constrained cost minimisation:

\mathcal{L} = \sum_{i} C_{i}(\tau_{i}^{*}) S_{i}
+ \sum_{i} \mu_{i}\bigl(\tau_{\min} - \mathrm{FLH}_{i}(S)\bigr)
+ \lambda\Bigl(D_{\max} - \sum_{i} S_{i}\Bigr).

Taking the derivative with respect to S_{i}, assuming for simplicity that the band boundaries are fixed, gives the first‑order condition

C_{i}(\tau_{i}^{*}) - \lambda - \mu_{i}\frac{\partial\mathrm{FLH}_{i}}{\partial S_{i}} = 0.

Because \partial\mathrm{FLH}_{i}/\partial S_{i} < 0 (adding capacity reduces utilisation), a binding constraint (\mu_{i}>0) effectively raises the cost coefficient of technology i. This shifts its cost line upward and pushes the breakpoint toward the neighbouring technology with a smaller shadow price. The shift \delta_{ij} is proportional to (\mu_{i} - \mu_{j})/(V_{j} - V_{i}). The piecewise‑linear structure of the envelope is preserved; the partition remains, but intervals are shortened for the constrained technologies. A full bilevel treatment confirms this essential behaviour. ∎

