Appendix C

Reliability Proofs and Copula Derivations

C.1 Independent Redundancy – Theorem 3

Let a duration band be served by m independent layers, each with availability A_{j} \in (0,1]. Failures are assumed independent. The probability that all layers fail simultaneously is

P_{\text{fail}} = \prod_{j=1}^{m} (1 - A_{j}).

Since each factor 1-A_{j} \in [0,1), adding a new layer with A_{m+1}>0 multiplies the product by a factor strictly less than one, so P_{\text{fail}} strictly decreases. If all layers have identical availability A, then P_{\text{fail}} = (1-A)^{m}, which decays exponentially with m. ∎

C.2 Common‑Cause Shock Model

Introduce a common‑cause shock that occurs with probability \gamma and fails all layers simultaneously. Conditional on no shock (probability 1-\gamma), failures occur independently with probabilities \lambda_{j} = 1 - A_{j}. The total failure probability is

P_{\text{fail}} = \gamma + (1-\gamma)\prod_{j=1}^{m} (1-\lambda_{j}).

As m \to \infty, if \lambda_{j} > 0, the product term vanishes and P_{\text{fail}} \to \gamma. Thus, for any \gamma > 0 the benefit of additional redundancy saturates. ∎

C.3 Gaussian Copula Model and Proposition 5.1

To model correlated failures, let the failure indicator X_{j} \in \{0,1\} have marginal failure probability q_{j} = 1 - A_{j}. We embed these margins in a Gaussian copula with correlation matrix \Sigma. Let Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma) and define

X_{j} = 1 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Z_{j} \leq \Phi^{-1}(q_{j}),

where \Phi is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The joint survival function (all layers operational) is then

P(\text{all up}) = \Phi_{m}\bigl(-\Phi^{-1}(q_{1}), \dots, -\Phi^{-1}(q_{m}); \Sigma\bigr),

with \Phi_{m} the m-variate normal CDF.

Proposition 5.1 (Diminishing returns under correlation).
For identical margins q = 1-A and exchangeable correlation \rho (i.e. \Sigma = (1-\rho)I + \rho\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\mathsf{T}}), the marginal reliability gain from adding an (m+1)-th layer is approximately \Delta R_{m+1} \approx (1-\rho) R_{m} A. The gain vanishes as \rho \to 1. There exists a critical correlation \rho_{\text{crit}} beyond which the cost of an additional layer exceeds the value of the outage reduction.

Heuristic derivation. Condition on the first m layers being operational, i.e. Z_{j} > u with u = \Phi^{-1}(q) for j=1,\dots,m. The conditional distribution of Z_{m+1} is normal with mean

\mu_{m+1|1:m} = \rho \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{m} u}{1 + (m-1)\rho},

and variance

\sigma^{2}_{m+1|1:m} = 1 - \frac{\rho^{2}m}{1 + (m-1)\rho}.

The conditional survival probability is

P(Z_{m+1} > u \mid \text{first } m > u) 
= 1 - \Phi\!\left(\frac{u - \mu_{m+1|1:m}}{\sigma_{m+1|1:m}}\right).

For small \rho or large m, this approaches (1-\rho)A. Hence the incremental gain is approximately (1-\rho) R_{m} A. Equating the annualised cost of the new layer with the reduction in expected outage cost yields \rho_{\text{crit}}. Rigorous proof requires numerical evaluation of the multivariate normal orthant probability; the case study in Chapter 8 provides such validation. ∎

C.4 Partial Substitutability and k-out-of-m Reliability

If each layer j can only supply a fraction \alpha_{j} of the band’s demand, the system survives if at least k out of m layers are operational. The failure probability becomes

P_{\text{fail}} = 1 - \sum_{l=k}^{m} \sum_{\substack{\text{subsets of size } l}} P(\text{exactly those } l \text{ layers up}),

where the joint probabilities may be evaluated using the same copula framework. The decoupling score \delta_{ij} = 1 - \rho_{ij} remains a practical tool for selecting technologies with minimal failure correlation.

