Appendix D

Exergy‑Aware Coordination and the Decomposition of \kappa_{\text{exergy}}

D.1 Power Balance with Conversion Losses

At the electrical interface, generation G(t), demand D(t), load shedding L(t), and storage charge/discharge must balance. Let p_{i}^{+}(t) be charging power to layer i, p_{i}^{-}(t) discharging power from layer i, and p_{i \to j}(t) the power sent from layer i to be converted into the energy form of layer j. Conversion efficiency \eta_{ij} \in (0,1] implies that delivering p_{i \to j}(t) to layer j requires an input of p_{i \to j}(t)/\eta_{ij} from the grid. The net additional load due to conversion is the difference between input and delivered power, summed over all ordered pairs (i,j) with i \neq j:

\sum_{i,j}\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{ij}} - 1\right)p_{i \to j}(t).

The global power balance therefore reads

\sum_{i} p_{i}^{-}(t) + L(t) = D(t) - G(t) + \sum_{i} p_{i}^{+}(t) + \sum_{i,j}\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{ij}} - 1\right)p_{i \to j}(t), \tag{D.1}

which is Equation (6.2) of the main text.

D.2 Definition of Benchmark Costs

We define three dispatch benchmarks, all respecting device and conversion efficiencies.

Structural ideal dispatch, C_{\text{struct}}.
The residual load duration curve is partitioned into the intervals dictated by the static envelope. Each interval is served exclusively by its designated technology; inter‑band energy transfers are forbidden. For each band, a dedicated sizing and dispatch optimisation minimises the primary exergy cost, i.e., the total energy purchased from generation multiplied by the shadow price of primary exergy, \lambda. The optimisation is a small mixed‑integer linear program for each band.

Perfect‑forecast dispatch, C_{\text{forecast}}.
The same band structure is used, but inter‑band transfers are allowed if they reduce total exergy cost. The system has perfect foresight of all exogenous variables (net load). The resulting problem is a large linear program with full temporal coupling.

Real dispatch, C_{\text{real}}.
The hierarchical model predictive control (MPC) described in §6.5 is simulated. Day‑ahead decisions are based on a stochastic program with 50 scenarios; intraday updates occur every 15 minutes; real‑time imbalances are handled by droop control and supercapacitors.

D.3 Decomposition of Coordination Efficiency

Coordination efficiency is decomposed as

\kappa_{\text{exergy}} = \kappa_{\text{forecast}} \times \kappa_{\text{struct}},

where

\kappa_{\text{forecast}} = \frac{C_{\text{forecast}}}{C_{\text{real}}}, \qquad
\kappa_{\text{struct}} = \frac{C_{\text{struct}}}{C_{\text{forecast}}}.

Interpretation. \kappa_{\text{struct}} measures the thermodynamic penalty of inter‑band transfers and dispatch imperfections under perfect information; it is always \le 1 because the forecast solution cannot outperform the structurally ideal benchmark if that benchmark already meets the load without transfers. \kappa_{\text{forecast}} isolates the value of improved prediction, capturing the cost increase due to forecast errors. In the Texas case study, we obtained C_{\text{struct}} = 2.15 bn $/yr, C_{\text{forecast}} = 2.21 bn $/yr, and C_{\text{real}} = 2.44 bn $/yr, yielding \kappa_{\text{struct}} = 0.97, \kappa_{\text{forecast}} = 0.91, and \kappa_{\text{exergy}} = 0.88. The small difference between C_{\text{struct}} and C_{\text{forecast}} arises because discrete capacity sizing can force minor inter‑band transfers; nevertheless the decomposition provides a diagnostic tool for system operators.

