Appendix E

Texas Case Study: Detailed Methodology

E.1 Data Sources

· ERCOT demand: Hourly load profiles from the Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) for 2019, weather‑normalised and scaled by an annual growth factor of 1.015 yr⁻¹ to represent electrification and population growth through 2050 (total increase 56%). Peak demand reaches \approx 120 GW.
· Wind and solar generation: Capacity factor time series from NREL’s WIND Toolkit and National Solar Radiation Data Base (NSRDB), spatially aggregated to the ERCOT region. Installed capacities are set at 150 GW wind (average capacity factor 38%) and 250 GW solar (average capacity factor 24%), yielding approximately 82 % annual renewable penetration before curtailment.
· Residual load: D_{\text{net}}(t) = \max(0,\, D(t) - G_{\text{wind}}(t) - G_{\text{solar}}(t)). The duration curve is constructed by sorting the 8760 hourly values in descending order. Sub‑hourly fluctuations for the supercapacitor band are synthesised from typical ramp‑rate distributions.
· Technology cost and performance: Baseline costs F_{i}, V_{i} are taken from Sepulveda et al. (2021) for 2050 projections, cross‑checked with DOE Storage Innovations 2030 (2022) and Lazard’s Levelized Cost of Storage analysis (2023). Learning rates are derived from McNerney et al. (2021) and the DOE report. Round‑trip efficiencies are as follows: supercapacitor 0.95; Li‑ion 0.92; flow battery 0.80; iron‑air 0.70; hydrogen (PEM electrolysis + storage + fuel cell) 0.36; synthetic fuel (power‑to‑liquid) 0.30. For the hydrogen and synthetic fuel pathways, compression (0.90) and AC/DC conversion (0.98) are included.

E.2 Iterative Sizing Algorithm with FLH Constraint

Because full‑load hours \mathrm{FLH}_{i} depend on the dispatch, which in turn depends on the installed capacities, an iterative bilevel procedure is employed:

1. Initialisation. Compute unconstrained breakpoints using t=0 cost data.
2. Band assignment. Each hour of the residual load duration curve is assigned to the technology whose duration interval contains that hour.
3. Initial sizing. For each technology, set
   S_{i} = \max\!\left( \text{peak net load in band } i,\; \frac{\text{total energy in band } i}{\tau_{i}^{*}} \right),
   where \tau_{i}^{*} is the width of the band, ensuring both power and energy adequacy.
4. Dispatch simulation. Solve a linear optimal dispatch program (hourly resolution, CPLEX) that minimises total generation cost subject to state‑of‑charge constraints, using the sized capacities. This yields annual discharged energies E_{i}.
5. FLH evaluation. \mathrm{FLH}_{i} = E_{i} / S_{i}.
6. Constraint check. If any \mathrm{FLH}_{i} < 1500\,\text{h}, reduce S_{i} by 5 % (or shift the breakpoint inward). Reclassify hours at the margins of the band: move the affected hours to the adjacent technology that has a higher FLH margin. Return to Step 3.
7. Iterate until all \mathrm{FLH}_{i} \ge 1500 or a maximum of 20 iterations is reached. Convergence was obtained in 8 iterations.

E.3 Baseline Comparisons

· Li‑ion only: All durations are served by Li‑ion batteries, sized to meet the same peak net load and total energy. The cost of providing long‑duration storage with Li‑ion is prohibitive, and the FLH constraint forces severe overbuild, making the system 22 % more expensive than the FESA portfolio (the main text reports 18 % based on slightly different reliability tolerances).
· Hydrogen only: Hydrogen is used for all durations exceeding one hour. High power‑conversion costs for short durations lead to extreme cost penalties; the FLH constraint further degrades economics.

E.4 Exergy‑Adjusted Duration Envelope

To incorporate thermodynamic quality directly into the technology selection, we define the exergy‑adjusted cost per unit of useful power capacity as

\widetilde{C}_{i}(\tau) = \frac{F_{i} + V_{i}\tau}{\eta_{i}},

where \eta_{i} is the round‑trip electrical efficiency. For long‑duration chemical storage, the simple synthetic fuel parameters in Table 8.1 (F=1000, V=0.5, \eta=0.30) represent the idealised fuel synthesis step only. A complete electricity‑to‑electricity pathway for synthetic fuel must include direct air capture of CO₂, additional compression, and combustion in a combined‑cycle turbine, yielding an effective round‑trip efficiency of approximately \eta \approx 0.12 and raising the energy‑capacity cost to V \approx 5 $/kWh (Pivovar et al., 2023; Gerloff, 2023). Using these refined parameters, the exergy‑adjusted cost for synthetic fuel at \tau = 2000\,\text{h} is

\widetilde{C}_{\text{syn}}(2000) = \frac{1000 + 5 \times 2000}{0.12} \approx 91,667\; \text{\$/kW‑yr},

whereas for hydrogen

\widetilde{C}_{\text{H}_{2}}(2000) = \frac{800 + 2 \times 2000}{0.36} \approx 13,333\; \text{\$/kW‑yr}.

Consequently, synthetic fuel is excluded from the exergy‑adjusted envelope. The resulting optimal partition contains five layers (supercapacitor, Li‑ion, flow battery, iron‑air, hydrogen), eliminating the seasonal synthetic fuel band. This result underscores the importance of an exergy‑aware perspective: a purely monetary screening curve can hide catastrophic thermodynamic inefficiencies.

E.5 Sensitivity of Reliability to Failure Correlation

The dynamic Gaussian copula model of §5.3 was evaluated for the 4–75 h band (Li‑ion, flow battery, hydrogen). The correlation parameter \rho was varied uniformly from 0 to 0.8 in steps of 0.1. The joint failure probability was computed by numerical integration of the multivariate normal survival function using Genz’s algorithm (Genz, 1992). Results:

· \rho = 0: P_{\text{fail}} = 0.07\%
· \rho = 0.2: P_{\text{fail}} = 0.09\%
· \rho = 0.4: P_{\text{fail}} = 0.15\%
· \rho = 0.6: P_{\text{fail}} = 0.35\%
· \rho = 0.8: P_{\text{fail}} = 1.2\%

The critical correlation for which P_{\text{fail}} exceeds 0.5 % is approximately \rho \approx 0.55, confirming that the diversity benefit is robust for the moderate correlation levels observed in practice.

