Appendix F

Stochastic Learning and Real Options Valuation

F.1 Derivation of the Cost Stochastic Differential Equations

Assume learning‑by‑doing: the unit cost c of a technology is related to cumulative production Q by a constant elasticity,

\frac{dc}{c} = -\alpha\,\frac{dQ}{Q}, \qquad \alpha \in (0,1).

For a deterministic exponential growth path Q(t) = Q_{0}e^{gt}, this yields the familiar experience curve c(t) = c_{0}(Q(t)/Q_{0})^{-\alpha} = c_{0}e^{-\alpha g t}. To capture uncertainty, we model Q as a geometric Brownian motion:

\frac{dQ}{Q} = \mu\,dt + \sigma\,dW.

Applying Itô’s lemma to the function c(Q) = c_{0}(Q/Q_{0})^{-\alpha} gives

dc = -\alpha c\,\frac{dQ}{Q} + \frac{1}{2}\alpha(\alpha+1)c\,\sigma^{2}dt.

For typical volatility levels (\sigma \ll 1), the second term is negligible compared to the drift. The dominant stochastic behaviour is therefore captured by the simplified SDEs

\frac{dF_{i}}{F_{i}} = -\alpha_{i}^{F}\frac{dQ_{i}}{Q_{i}} + \sigma_{i}^{F}dW_{t}^{F},\qquad
\frac{dV_{i}}{V_{i}} = -\alpha_{i}^{V}\frac{dQ_{i}}{Q_{i}} + \sigma_{i}^{V}dW_{t}^{V},

as used in the main text.

F.2 Real Options Bellman Equation

At each planning interval \Delta t, the infrastructure manager decides whether to invest in convertible assets. Let the state be the vector of costs \mathbf{F}, \mathbf{V} and the existing portfolio \mathbf{S}. The value function satisfies

V(t, \mathbf{F}, \mathbf{V}, \mathbf{S}) = \max_{u \in \{0,1\}^{n}}\;
\Bigl\{ -C(u) + e^{-r\Delta t}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[ V(t+\Delta t, \mathbf{F}', \mathbf{V}', \mathbf{S}') \mid \mathbf{F}, \mathbf{V}, \mathbf{S} \bigr] \Bigr\},

where u indicates which technologies receive convertible investment, C(u) is the incremental capital cost, and the expectation is taken over the stochastic learning processes. At the final time T, the value is the salvage value of the portfolio. This dynamic program can be solved numerically by regression Monte Carlo (Longstaff & Schwartz, 2001).

The adaptive optionality fraction \Omega is defined as the ratio of convertible capacity to total capacity. Convertible capacity comprises assets that can switch storage medium (e.g., caverns sized for multiple gases) or be repurposed at less than 20 % of their original capital cost. High \Omega ensures that the architecture can follow the evolving cost envelope without stranding assets.

